  },
  keywords = {TCA-reception, communicative-rationality, lifeworld-system, retrospective, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{fultner2001intelligibility,
  author = {Fultner, Barbara},
  title = {Intelligibility and Conflict Resolution in the Lifeworld},
  journal = {Continental Philosophy Review},
  year = {2001},
  volume = {34},
  number = {4},
  pages = {419--436},
  doi = {10.1023/a:1013130508004},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Fultner argues that Habermas's concept of the lifeworld is too amorphous to bear the
  theoretical weight he places on it. He conceives it both as diffuse and holistic (a background of
  taken-for-granted presuppositions) and as structured (a set of specific cultural, social, and personal
  components). These conceptions are in tension: the lifeworld as totality cannot explain why its structural
  components -- cultural reproduction, social integration, socialization -- provide the resources for resolving
  communicative conflict. In the end, Habermas presupposes the very mutual intelligibility the lifeworld is
  meant to explain.

  RELEVANCE: Pinpoints the conceptual difficulty at the center of the lifeworld's function in TCA -- it is
  both the resource for and the product of communicative action. This circular structure is crucial for
  evaluating the colonization thesis, since the thesis requires that the lifeworld have determinate content
  that can be threatened.

  POSITION: Immanent critique of the lifeworld concept's internal coherence; challenges its explanatory role.
  },
  keywords = {lifeworld, intelligibility, conflict-resolution, critique, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{flynn2014system,
  author = {Flynn, Jeffrey},
  title = {System and Lifeworld in Habermas' Theory of Democracy},
  journal = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {40},
  number = {2},
  pages = {205--214},
  doi = {10.1177/0191453713518326},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Flynn defends the system/lifeworld model against Hugh Baxter's argument that it cannot
  make conceptual space for democratic politics in Habermas's later legal theory (Between Facts and Norms).
  Flynn challenges Baxter's claim that the understanding of "system" shifts significantly from TCA to BFN,
  arguing that the two-level model remains coherent and that law can function as the medium through which
  communicative power from the lifeworld constrains system imperatives.

  RELEVANCE: Part of a debate (Flynn, Wirts, and Baxter in a Philosophy & Social Criticism symposium) about
  whether the lifeworld/system distinction survives Habermas's turn to law and democracy. Important for
  assessing the continuity between TCA and Habermas's later political-legal theory.

  POSITION: Defense of the lifeworld/system dualism's coherence in Habermas's later democratic theory.
  },
  keywords = {lifeworld, system, democracy, law, Between-Facts-and-Norms, Medium},
  abstract = {In this article I challenge two arguments central to Hugh Baxter's critical interpretation of Habermas in his recent book, Habermas: The Discourse Theory of Law and Democracy (2011). Both arguments focus on whether Habermas' system-lifeworld model of society can successfully make space for democratic politics. Baxter highlights problems with both Habermas' The Theory of Communicative Action [hereafter cited as TCA] and Habermas' attempts to fix those problems in Between Facts and Norms [hereafter cited as BFN]. Thus, engaging Baxter on these issues provides an opportunity to think about how to read two of Habermas' most significant texts in relation to each other. Baxter stresses discontinuities but I draw attention to important continuities. The first discontinuity highlighted by Baxter is between TCA's functionalism and BFN's normative approach. By contrast, in section I my article stresses elements of TCA that have been under-appreciated in general and by Baxter in particular, challenging us to rethink aspects of the standard reading of functionalist versus normative moments in the trajectory of Habermas' thought. More specifically, in the second discontinuity Baxter identifies in BFN a significant shift in Habermas' understanding of the nature of a 'system'. I challenge this interpretation in section II and provide what I think is a superior reconstruction of the model presented in BFN.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{wirts2014defense,
  author = {Wirts, Amelia M.},
  title = {A Defense of the Lifeworld},
  journal = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {40},
  number = {2},
  pages = {215--223},
  doi = {10.1177/0191453713518328},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Wirts argues, against Baxter's critique, that Habermas must retain the theoretical
  distinction between system and lifeworld even if in everyday practice they are not neatly disentangled.
  Without the lifeworld concept, there would be no source of legitimacy for law: communicative power requires
  a domain of interaction governed by communicative norms that is distinguishable from strategic-instrumental
  domains. The distinction's normative function is irreducible to its empirical application.

  RELEVANCE: Engages the question of whether the system/lifeworld dualism is theoretically sustainable
  under the pressures identified by critics. The defense illuminates what is genuinely at stake normatively
  in maintaining the distinction -- a concern central to Habermas's diagnosis of modernity's pathologies.

  POSITION: Defense of the lifeworld/system distinction's normative indispensability.
  },
  keywords = {lifeworld, system, legitimacy, law, defense, Medium},
  abstract = {Hugh Baxter's book Habermas: A Discourse Theory of Law and Democracy not only carefully recounts Habermas' political and legal theory, but also raises several insightful criticisms of Habermas. Of particular note is Baxter's criticism of Habermas' system-lifeworld model originally presented in Theory of Communicative Action. Baxter argues that Habermas ought to discard the concept of the lifeworld because the distinction between lifeworld and system is no longer tenable in the model of political power presented in Habermas' later work, Between Facts and Norms, which focuses more explicitly on law as a social system. Baxter contends that the lifeworld, which Habermas describes as those parts of our lives that are structured by communicative action and shared norms, is an unhelpful way of understanding our social world because, in reality, most of our interactions are structured both by communicative norms and by the strategic action typical of 'the system'. I argue, however, that Habermas must retain the theoretical distinction between system and lifeworld, though in everyday practice they are not so neatly disentangled, because without such an element there would be no source of legitimacy for the law.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{harrington1998lifeworld,
  author = {Harrington, Austin},
  title = {Habermas' Concept of the Lifeworld},
  journal = {Social Philosophy Today},
  year = {1998},
  volume = {13},
  pages = {39--53},
  doi = {10.5840/socphiltoday19981320},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Harrington provides a synoptic defense of the lifeworld concept against critics who argue
  it is either incoherent (Bohman) or ideologically blind (Fraser). He traces the concept's origins in
  Husserl and Schutz and argues that Habermas's transformation of it into a sociological concept retains
  its phenomenological force while giving it the intersubjective and communicative grounding it lacks in
  its predecessors. The lifeworld is best understood as the structure of shared presuppositions that makes
  mutual understanding possible and that is reproduced through communicative action.

  RELEVANCE: Contextualizes the lifeworld concept in the phenomenological tradition and defends its
  coherence. Important for the debate about whether Habermas's sociological use of "lifeworld" is faithful
  to or distorts its phenomenological origins (Husserl, Schutz).

  POSITION: Interpretive defense of the lifeworld concept and its transformation from Husserlian
  phenomenology to Habermasian social theory.
  },
  keywords = {lifeworld, phenomenology, Husserl, Schutz, defense, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{blau2019rationality,
  author = {Blau, Adrian},
  title = {Habermas on Rationality: Means, Ends and Communication},
  journal = {European Journal of Political Theory},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {21},
  number = {2},
  pages = {321--344},
  doi = {10.1177/1474885119867679},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Blau offers a constructive critique of Habermas's account of means-ends (instrumental)
  rationality, arguing that Habermas and critical theorists caricature it as necessarily egocentric. Blau
  distinguishes sincerity and autonomy -- rather than non-egocentrism -- as the key features that differentiate
  communicative rationality from instrumental rationality. This redraws the boundary between the two types
  in a way that is more philosophically defensible and preserves the essential Habermasian insight without
  relying on the problematic characterization of means-ends reasoning.

  RELEVANCE: Recent analytic-philosophical engagement with the communicative/instrumental distinction that
  is central to TCA. Blau's critique and proposed revision both challenge and refine the typology that
  grounds Habermas's diagnosis of modernity's rationalization pathologies.

  POSITION: Revisionary critique that preserves communicative rationality while correcting the
  characterization of instrumental rationality.
  },
  keywords = {communicative-rationality, instrumental-rationality, means-ends, critique, Medium},
  abstract = {This is a constructive critique of Habermas's account of rationality, which is central to his political theory and has sparked theoretical and empirical research across academia. Habermas and many critical theorists caricature means-ends rationality (the ability to pick good means to ends), e.g. by wrongly depicting it as egocentric. This weakens Habermas's attempt to distinguish means-ends rationality from his hugely important and influential idea of communicative rationality (roughly, the rationality of genuine discussion). I suggest that sincerity and autonomy, rather than non-egocentrism, are the key distinguishing features of communicative rationality. This shows that communicative rationality actually overlaps with means-ends rationality. Indeed, means-ends rationality is needed by critical theorists, as I exemplify by showing its use in deliberative democracy. Moreover, means-ends rationality will be present in discourse ethics, as I show with the example of moral discourse about gay marriage. My article thus challenges decades of what Habermas and critical theorists have written on means-ends and communicative rationality, but I stay broadly true to -- and hopefully improve -- Habermas's account of rationality.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

