  using reason to undermine all reason.

  RELEVANCE: This essay, along with the parallel TCA chapter, is Habermas'
  principal statement of his disagreement with the first generation and is
  the primary source for understanding his claim that negative dialectics is
  a performative contradiction. It provides the textual basis for the domain's
  central question: what, for Habermas, went wrong in Adorno and Horkheimer.

  POSITION: Represents Habermas' critique of first-generation Frankfurt School
  from the standpoint of communicative rationality.
  },
  keywords = {Habermas, Adorno, Horkheimer, Dialectic-of-Enlightenment, communicative-rationality, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{marcuse1964onedimensional,
  author = {Marcuse, Herbert},
  title = {One-Dimensional Man: Studies in the Ideology of Advanced Industrial Society},
  year = {1964},
  publisher = {Beacon Press},
  address = {Boston},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Marcuse argues that advanced industrial society has achieved
  a form of total integration: the mechanisms of mass consumption, the culture
  industry, and the operationalist logic of science and technology eliminate
  the "second dimension" of transcendence and negation, producing a
  one-dimensional consciousness incapable of imagining alternatives to the
  existing order. Unlike Horkheimer and Adorno, Marcuse ties this diagnosis
  explicitly to the critique of technology as ideology.

  RELEVANCE: Habermas takes Marcuse's concept of technology-as-ideology as a
  starting point but disagrees with its theoretical grounding. In "Technology
  and Science as 'Ideology'" (1968/1970), Habermas argues that Marcuse wrongly
  treats all technology as inherently ideological; Habermas instead distinguishes
  purposive-rational action (technical) from communicative action (normative).
  The extent to which Habermas preserves and revises Marcuse's one-dimensionality
  diagnosis is central to understanding his debt to and departure from the
  first generation.

  POSITION: First-generation Frankfurt School; technology-critique strand that
  Habermas both draws on and substantially qualifies.
  },
  keywords = {Marcuse, one-dimensional-society, technology, Frankfurt-School, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{honneth1991critiquepower,
  author = {Honneth, Axel},
  title = {The Critique of Power: Reflective Stages in a Critical Social Theory},
  year = {1991},
  publisher = {MIT Press},
  address = {Cambridge, MA},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Honneth surveys the tradition from Horkheimer and Adorno
  through Habermas and Foucault, arguing that each stage of critical theory
  has failed to adequately theorize social conflict as action-theoretically
  grounded. He critiques Habermas specifically for relying on a
  systems-theoretic account (borrowed from Luhmann) that occludes the
  intersubjective dynamics through which social domination is reproduced and
  contested. The path forward, Honneth argues, requires a social theory that
  keeps conflict and recognition at its center.

  RELEVANCE: This is Honneth's founding statement of his differences with
  Habermas and his diagnosis of what Habermas' systems/lifeworld dualism
  misses. It provides the theoretical motivation for the recognition turn and
  is the key text for understanding how the third generation of Frankfurt
  School thought inherits from and departs from Habermas' account of modernity.

  POSITION: Third-generation Frankfurt School; foundational for the recognition
  turn critique of Habermas' communicative-action framework.
  },
  keywords = {Honneth, Habermas, critique-of-power, recognition, Frankfurt-School, High},
  abstract = {Mihaela Fistioc’sThe Beautiful Shape of the Goodbuilds a case that Plato and, ultimately, Pythagoras, helped inspire Kant’sCritique of the Power of Judgment. Apart from this exercise in the history of philosophy, she offers an engaging study of the relation between the beautiful and the moral, and the centrality in our lives of aesthetic experience.
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

We respond with pleasure to beauty in the world, whether it be of natural or of human origin, and we expect others to respond likewise. Both of these facts about us are cause for some wonder, since one might just as readily imagine a race of beings emotionally indifferent to their surroundings. And who would suspect that something as private as one’s own personal responses of pleasure would demand such high claims on objectivity that we hold others to experience the world similarly? Yet wedoroutinely encounter this intersubjective agreement of pleasure—both as a demand and in fact—all of which suggests a deep linkage between our aesthetic impulse and the world around us. The beautiful, the good, and the surprising fit between the world and our desire to understand are brought together in Kant’sCritique of the Power of Judgment(1790). In the current book under review, a lightly re-worked doctoral dissertation (Chicago, 2000), these Kantian themes are explored and traced back to Greece in chapters on (1) Kant’s exposure to Pythagorean and Platonic thought, (2) Plato’sSymposiumdiscussion of love, beauty, and the ascent to virtue, and (3) Kant’s parallel attempts in the thirdCritiqueof relating beauty and the good. While linked thematically, these chapters read nearly like separate studies, and I will discuss them as such.

In chapter one, Fistioc admits the lack of clear evidence that Kant read any of Plato’s dialogues (for instance, none were listed in his estate library, nor is the internal evidence compelling), although she notes his likely access to Latin translations at the university library where he served as an assistant librarian (1766-72), as well as to the German translations being published in the early 1780s. But apart from any direct acquaintance Kant may have had with the dialogues, we also know he was familiar with Jakob Brucker’s famousHistoria critica philosophiae(Leipzig, 1742-44; 2nd ed: 1767)—Brucker is cited in theCritique of Pure Reason(A 316/B 342)—and Fistioc offers a suggestive account of how Kant might have acquired here much of his understanding of Plato and Pythagoras. To explore this possibility, she outlines Brucker’s discussion (pp. 12-35), highlighting Pythagoras’s use of symbolism, his discovery of the mathematical ratios of the harmonic scales (and specifically, the connection between pleasure and form), and the use of beauty in the moral education of the soul. This last point is built around a brief comment appearing in several versions of Kant’s logic lectures (L2,Vienna, andJäsche) that Pythagoras “moderated or purified religion of its folly, introducing lawfulness”—as though the lawfulness of mathematics eventually migrated into the very lives of the mathematicians.

It is of some interest that Brucker’s history devoted more pages to Pythagoras than it did to Plato (120 and 103, respectively), and a few of Kant’s reviews of the history of philosophy are similar in this respect (although overall, Kant refers to Plato rather more often than he does to Pythagoras). Kant seemed especially taken by the Pythagorean understanding of the soul as a self-moving number, a notion listed in Brucker’s index (under ‘anima’), and which then appears in several of Kant’s texts: in his essay “On a Newly Arisen Superior Tone in Philosophy” of 1796 (Ak. 8: 392), but also in the K2 metaphysics notes (early 1790s; Ak. 28: 753) and the Blomberg logic notes (early 1770s; Ak. 24: 36). The suggestion here is that Pythagoras was connecting the form of the mathematical ratios of various musical tones with the pleasure elicited by these formal features. The form is experienced as beautiful, thus filling the soul with pleasure. Music can affect the soul because both are a kind of form— thus, form affecting form. But now this mystery: Why should one mathematical ratio evoke pleasure, and another not?

Kant also saw Pythagoras and Plato as exemplars of the deep human interest in metaphysics, with the root error arising from an illicit coupling of mathematics with metaphysics. Success in the former made them overly confident in the latter, making use of the same method and hoping to be rewarded with similar .prioriintuitions — this cautionary tale is found throughout Kant’s metaphysics lectures, as well as in the “Doctrine of Method” of theCritique of Pure Reason(1781) and in the “Superior Tone” essay (1796).

One small fault with this otherwise engaging chapter lies with an undue bother over the dating of Kant’s first encounter with Pythagoras and Plato. The goal here, of course, was to strengthen the plausibility that a central concern of theCritique of the Power of Judgmentfound its inspiration in Pythagoras, and thus that Kant’s awareness of Pythagoras pre-dates the composition of theCritique. To this end, the author made nice use of some of the metaphysics and logic student notes (pp. 49-56); but noticing that a discussion of Pythagoras also appears in theBlomberglogic notes (early 1770s; Ak. 24: 31-36) would have saved the author and the reader some trouble. At the same time, given her concern with the genesis of Kant’s ideas, it is unfortunate that she neglected the extensive discussions on pleasure, taste, and the beautiful that can be found in the “empirical psychology” section of the metaphysics notes, ranging from the earlyL1notes of the mid-1770s to the lateVigilantiusnotes of 1794-95.

Chapter two provides a useful analysis of Plato’sSymposium, connecting the discussion of love with the Pythagorean insight that certain patterns are a source of pleasure. Fistioc presents theSymposiumas an allegory of the human condition and as an inquiry into the best way to live, with each participant representing a different stage of understanding or moral development, and each guided, after his own fashion, by love (eros). Love as a longing for oneness, described in Aristophanes’ speech as forever foundering on human brokenness, becomes able—in the Myth of Diotima—to make humans once again whole. In this myth, as recounted by Socrates, love involves not only need, but also the resourcefulness to free us from a doomed, blind groping after wholeness. We are initially attracted to others by an outer or lesser beauty, but what we desire in the end is wisdom and the good. First we love a body, but then we discover that the beauty we love is not in this or that body as such, but rather in their form or symmetry, thus cracking open a door into the universal. And while in love with this particular, we also discover for the first time a selfless concern for the beloved, and so a concern for the other in general, thus developing an understanding of the social laws—in Kantian terms, we become able to make these laws our own, and thus to become self-legislators. We are attracted by something offering both pleasure and understanding, and this we find in a form—at first physical, but later more abstract. Beauty becomes a kind of lawfulness; to experience beauty is to respond with pleasure to the discovery of a right proportion or intelligible form. Or as Fistioc sums it up, “When in love one has new eyes, and with these new eyes one can see the structure of the world in a way one could not see it before” (p. 95).

This reading of Plato prepares us for her interpretation of Kant’sCritique of the Power of Judgment, which she develops in the third chapter. The Pythagoreans discovered that certain mathematical ratios resulted in pleasing forms, and in this chapter Fistioc argues that Kant’s account of beauty rests principally on an analysis of this pleasurable response to form. The upshot of her reading of this analysis, as I understood it, is as follows: Weare ableto find meaning in the world by regularly inserting purposiveness (viewing things as though they came from the hand of a designing intelligence), and wewantto find meaning in the world because doing so gives us pleasure; what is more, we often discover thatothersfind the same meaning that we do. In laying out her case, Fistioc finds a good fit between Plato’s notion of love (as developed primarily in the “Myth of Diotima”) and Kant’s reflecting power of judgment (as developed in the thirdCritique): both of these motivate and guide us, and ultimately point beyond to the moral order.},
}

@book{honneth1995struggle,
  author = {Honneth, Axel},
  title = {The Struggle for Recognition: The Moral Grammar of Social Conflicts},
  year = {1995},
  publisher = {Polity Press},
  address = {Cambridge},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Drawing on Hegel's early Jena writings and G.H. Mead's social
  psychology, Honneth argues that social conflicts are fundamentally driven by
  experiences of disrespect and misrecognition across three spheres: love, law,
  and social esteem. This "moral grammar" of conflict provides both a social
  theory of motivation and a normative framework for critical theory, grounding
  normativity in the experiences of subjects rather than in abstract
  communicative procedures.

  RELEVANCE: This book marks Honneth's most decisive departure from Habermas:
  where Habermas grounds critical theory in the procedural conditions of
  communicative rationality, Honneth grounds it in the phenomenology of
  recognition and disrespect. The contrast between these two approaches is
  central to evaluating what is preserved and lost in the shift from Habermas'
  communicative account of modernity to Honneth's recognition-theoretic one.

  POSITION: Third-generation Frankfurt School; recognition theory as alternative
  normative foundation to Habermas' discourse ethics.
  },
  keywords = {Honneth, recognition, social-conflict, Frankfurt-School, High},
  abstract = {It was a struggle, but commonsense won out. That’s not my verdict on Scott Soames’The Struggle for Modality.[1]That’s his verdict on the development of analytic philosophyvis-à-visour understanding of modality—a developmental arc described by Soames which culminates in our community embrace of metaphysical modality, specifically the distinction of essence and accidentin re. We began benighted because we made a crippling identification of necessity with analyticity and because we lacked a developed modal logic—the lack being partly a consequence of the crippling identification already made. Modal logic didn’t come of age until Kripke published his model-theoretic semantics for modal logic in the early 60s. But that wasn’t itself the decisive breakthrough because models need to be interpreted.
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

What was decisive was Kripke’s insight, which he developed in his 1970 lectures that were later published asNaming and Necessity(1980), that we each have pre-theoretic beliefs about which properties of a thing are essential and which accidental. They are beliefs that have nothing to do with analyticity; they are about properties, which things have regardless of how they are described. And they are beliefs that have the same epistemic status as the commonsense beliefs which G.E. Moore famously held we know with certainty are true, beliefs which cannot be undermined by philosophical argument. Now, because we’ve recognised that it’s only commonsense, essentialism, conceived in terms of metaphysical modality, has a default status for us.

In the struggle for modality, Quine is cast by Soames as a sceptic who denies the intelligibility of the distinction between essential and accidental properties. We might think of Kripke, in Soames’ version of history, as standing to Quine as Moore stood to Bradley in the struggle for realism. For Bradley denied the reality of time, whilst Moore pointed out that it was only commonsense to think that our bodies were born at a certain time in the past and have existed continuously ever since.

Soames is confident that history has led us to where we should be. In fact, Soames’ version of history has historical significance of its own as the expression of a contemporaryWeltanschauungwhich informs a certain metaphysical cast of mind. It’s unsurprising that Gideon Rosen should have appealed to Soames’ version of history as support for his thinking that the project of rehabilitating traditional notions of necessity and possibility ‘is now more or less complete’, a project whose value is ‘beyond dispute’’ (2010, 134). It's because he thinks that project now pretty much completed that Rosen thinks it’s time to take the next step along the metaphysical path and embrace the metaphysics of grounding—at one with the drive of history as Soames has described it. Rosen is referring to Soames’Philosophical Analysis in the Twentieth Century(2003). But this earlier work overlaps in several respects withThe Struggle for Modality,including the resuscitation of metaphysical modality—which is only to be expected, since six chapters from the latter are revised and updated versions of six chapters of the former. Whether Soames would himself be ready to take that next step with Rosen and embrace grounding, I can’t say—not from readingThe Struggle for Modalityanyway. But Rosen’s appeal to Soames’ version of history should serve as a reminder that it’s important to get history right, or as close to right as the multiform and inherently perspectival character of history allows. Because what’s received as history also influences what comes next.

What’s my verdict onThe Struggle for Modality? What I’ve described is the general sweep of Soames’ version of the history. But I don’t mean to imply that’s all there is to it. InThe Struggle for Modality, Soames begins by describing the emergence of modal logic in the works of C.I. Lewis, its development by Barcan, Quine’s misguided animadversions on the intelligibility of quantified modal logic, Carnap’s systematic but ultimately flawed semantics ofMeaning and Necessity(1947), and modal logic’s coming of age with Kripke’s semantics. Soames next shifts his focus away from modalityper sein order to reconstruct and assess ‘Two Dogmas of Empiricism’ (1951), Grice and Strawson’s ‘In Defence of a Dogma’ (1956) and the Carnap-Quine dispute, arguing that their holistic verificationism was a mistake. This is followed by a critique of Quine’s doctrine of indeterminacy of translation, which Soames argues is self-refuting. Soames then brings modality back centre stage to explain the contribution of Kripke’sNaming and Necessityto our understanding of metaphysical modality, defending Kripke’s ideas against his critics, including two-dimensionalists, and improving his arguments where Soames judges them in need of improvement. The remainder of the book is devoted to an account of ‘cognitive propositions’, which Soames introduces to meet the challenge he describes Kripke as having thrown down in ‘Puzzle about Belief’ (1979), to explain ordinary belief attributions and to apply Kripke’s ideas fromNaming and Necessityto new arenas, including extending Kripke’s analysis of names to numerals designating numbers.

Even from this summary, it should be clear thatThe Struggle for Modalityisn’t only about the struggle for modality but much more. Nor is it only about the history of analytic philosophy. It’s a book in which Soames forthrightly presents his own arguments and his own views on a great range of topics. This coheres with his stated aim to do philosophy together with the leading figures of 20thcentury analytic philosophy—‘to assist us in making progress with our unsolved problems’ (xvi). But it’s not an exacting scholarly study devoted to a minute inspection and interpretation of the primary texts in their historicalsitu. Nor is it a work which draws upon the numerous other scholarly studies of the period that do. Now, I don’t think this is necessarily a bad thing for a history of philosophy. It certainly is possible to lose sight of the animating spirit of an idea among the trees. But it is also possible to get the philosophical lessons from history wrong because the historical scholarship falls short. My verdict is thatThe Struggle for Modalityfalls short in this respect. For lack of scholarship, it fails in its stated aim of bringing the leading figures of 20th-century analytic philosophy into conversation with us because it listens to only part of what they had to say.

Let’s go back to the grand narrative arc of Soames’ version of history. Kripke won because Kripke’s essentialism was only commonsense, whereas Quine lost because Quine was just a sceptic about something that was only commonsense. But this is a misrepresentation of the historical Quine. Quine didn’t deny the intelligibility of the commonsense distinction between essence and accident. He held the distinction to be clear and serviceable but occasion dependent. As he explained in ‘Intensions Revisited’ (1977), ‘Relative to a particular inquiry, some predicates may play a more basic role than others, or may apply more fixedly; and these may be treated as essential’ (1977, 10). Because relative to another inquiry, the same predicates may be treated as accidental, ‘essence’ and ‘accident’ no more have a place in a logically regimented science designed to limn the general traits of reality than other occasion-dependent locutions such as ‘I’, ‘you’, ‘here’ and ‘now’. This was to be an enduring doctrine for Quine, manifest in several of his later writings, including one of his last, ‘Promoting Extensionality’ (1994).

Since Quine acknowledged a place for the commonsense distinction between essence and accident, in the pragmatics of inquiry, Quine cannot be simply dismissed Moore-style as a sceptic about something that’s only commonsense. On this point, it’s important to remember what the historical Moore actually said. He concluded ‘A Defence of Commonsense’ (1925) by reflecting about the commonsensical propositions he’d identified, ‘Of thetruthof these propositions there seems to me to be no doubt, but as to what is the correct analysis of them there seems to me to be the gravest doubt’ (1925: 228). In Moore’s terms, Quine isn’t denying the commonsense distinction between essence and accident but offering ananalysiswhich, because it’s an analysis ofit, cannot be ruled out solely on the basis that the distinction is commonsense.},
}

@article{muellerDoohm2015member,
  author = {M{\"u}ller-Doohm, Stefan},
  title = {Member of a school or exponent of a paradigm? {J}{\"u}rgen {H}abermas and critical theory},
  journal = {European Journal of Social Theory},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {20},
  number = {2},
  pages = {252--274},
  doi = {10.1177/1368431015622049},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Habermas' biographer examines the tension between Habermas'
  institutional identity as a Frankfurt School member and his theoretical
  identity as the originator of a distinct paradigm of communicative rationality.
  Muller-Doohm argues that Habermas belongs to the Frankfurt School in terms of
  institutional location and emancipatory intent, but represents a paradigm shift
  away from the philosophy of consciousness, negative dialectics, and the
  aestheticization of critique that characterized the first generation.

  RELEVANCE: This article is directly relevant to the research project's
  question about Habermas' self-understanding and his situation within the
  critical theory tradition. It draws on archival and biographical sources
  unavailable to earlier commentators and contextualizes the theoretical debates
  within the institutional history of the Institute for Social Research.

  POSITION: Intellectual-biographical approach; critical theory tradition from
  institutional history perspective.
  },
  keywords = {Habermas, Frankfurt-School, intellectual-history, critical-theory, Medium},
  abstract = {The label ‘Frankfurt School’ became popular in the ‘positivism dispute’ in the mid-1960s, but this article shows that it is wrong to describe Jürgen Habermas as representing a ‘second generation’ of exponents of critical theory. His communication theory of society is intended not as a transformation of, but as an alternative to, the older tradition of thought represented by Adorno and Horkheimer. The novel and innovative character of Habermas’s approach is demonstrated in relation to three thematic complexes: (1) the public sphere and language; (2) democracy and the constitutional state; and (3) system and lifeworld as categories for a theory of modernity.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{muellerDoohm2023adorno,
  author = {M{\"u}ller-Doohm, Stefan},
  title = {Adorno and {H}abermas: Two varieties of post-metaphysical thinking},
  journal = {European Journal of Social Theory},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {26},
  number = {3},
  pages = {391--407},
  doi = {10.1177/13684310231151313},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Despite their differences, both Adorno and Habermas are
  committed to a post-metaphysical philosophy that renounces strong ontological
  claims while maintaining philosophy's relevance as interpreter and critic.
  Muller-Doohm reconstructs areas of genuine convergence (anti-foundationalism,
  the "fall of metaphysics," concern with religious semantics) alongside the
  substantive difference: Adorno's negative dialectics treats the fall of
  metaphysics as an irresolvable aporia, while Habermas' post-metaphysical
  thinking reconstructs a modest practical reason as placeholder for metaphysical
  functions.

  RELEVANCE: This 2023 article provides a nuanced comparison of Adorno and
  Habermas that resists both the "total break" narrative and the facile
  assimilation of their projects. It is particularly useful for examining what
  Habermas preserves from Adorno's critique of identitarian thinking while
  transforming its theoretical form.

  POSITION: Comparative philosophical analysis; post-metaphysical thinking
  as shared framework with distinct realizations.
  },
  keywords = {Adorno, Habermas, post-metaphysical, Frankfurt-School, High},
  abstract = {This essay asks how much of Adorno is present in Habermas’s theory of communicative reason and how far Adorno anticipated Habermas in his linguistic-philosophical reflections. Despite all their differences, Adorno and Habermas agree that a contemporary philosophy must be conceived as a critique of metaphysics, which they develop with different theoretical means. For Adorno’s anti-idealist philosophy of negative dialectics, the ‘fall of metaphysics’ is irresistible, but he continues to reflect whether philosophy must still take account of the need for metaphysics. For Habermas, a modestly reconceived post-metaphysical philosophy, whose genealogy he reconstructs in his late work Auch eine Geschichte der Philosophie, is a placeholder for a theory of practical reason. As a hermeneutic science, philosophy also continues to pursue the task of contributing to human beings’ understanding of themselves and the world. Its role as an interpreter also crucially includes the attempt to translate the unsatisfied semantic elements of religious traditions into secular conceptions. This intention is in line with Adorno’s postulate that theological elements can only be sustained if they are transformed into this-worldly language.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{freyenhagen2023linguistic,
  author = {Freyenhagen, Fabian},
  title = {The Linguistic Turn in the Early {F}rankfurt {S}chool: {H}orkheimer and {A}dorno},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {61},
  number = {1},
  pages = {127--148},
  doi = {10.1353/hph.2023.0005},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Freyenhagen challenges the standard narrative that Habermas'
  linguistic-pragmatic turn represents an entirely new departure from the first
  generation. By analyzing Horkheimer's Eclipse of Reason, he argues that
  Horkheimer and Adorno share with the later Wittgenstein a picture of language
  as constitutively tied to forms of life, making language a repository of
  "objective reason" that resists full instrumental colonization. This allows
  a reconstruction of their theory that answers Habermas' charge that the first
  generation lacked normative resources for critique.

  RELEVANCE: This article directly challenges the domain's central narrative
  by arguing that Habermas' critique of the first generation misreads Horkheimer
  and Adorno's actual position on language and normativity. It is essential
  reading for a balanced assessment of what Habermas correctly diagnoses and
  where his reconstruction of the first generation is contested.

  POSITION: Defense of first-generation Frankfurt School against Habermas'
  critique; revisionist account of the linguistic-normative resources in
  Horkheimer and Adorno.
  },
  keywords = {Adorno, Horkheimer, Habermas, negative-dialectics, language, High},
  abstract = {abstract:Was there a linguistic turn in Frankfurt School Critical Theory before Habermas's communications-theoretic one? Might later Wittgenstein and the early Frankfurt School have adopted similar pictures of language? I propose that both questions should be answered affirmatively, focusing on Horkheimer's Eclipse of Reason. I argue that, thanks to the picture of language that Horkheimer and Adorno share with (later) Wittgenstein, we can reconstruct their theory in a way that renders it more defensible. Insofar as the human life form and language are inseparable, language can be an inextinguishable reservoir of what Horkheimer called "objective reason." Recognizing this allows us to answer Habermas's critique of Horkheimer and Adorno. Moreover, paying attention to this inseparableness can enable us to engage in disclosing social critique (for example, regarding current debates about sustainability).},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{kamper2025domination,
  author = {K{\"a}mper, Felix},
  title = {Domination by nature: On an unresolved tension in the {F}rankfurt {S}chool},
  journal = {Thesis Eleven},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {193},
  number = {1},
  pages = {91--106},
  doi = {10.1177/07255136251331986},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The article identifies two contrasting interpretations of the
  central reversal in Dialectic of Enlightenment — the claim that domination
  over nature leads to domination by nature. One reading (attributed partly to
  Habermas) treats this reversal as a contingent feature of specifically modern
  capitalist societies; another treats it as a general feature of human history.
  Kamper argues that Horkheimer and Adorno deliberately leave this tension
  unresolved, and that both Habermas and Honneth try to settle it toward their
  preferred frameworks in ways that distort the original text's critical force.

  RELEVANCE: This 2025 article provides a precise analysis of a specific crux
  in the Habermas-versus-first-generation debate, illuminating what is at stake
  in Habermas' decision to treat the pathologies of modernity as contingent
  and correctable (through communicative rationality) rather than as structural
  features of Enlightenment rationality itself.

  POSITION: Critical engagement with multiple generations of Frankfurt School;
  defends irreducible tension in Dialectic of Enlightenment against systematic
  reconstruction.
  },
  keywords = {Horkheimer, Adorno, Habermas, Honneth, nature-domination, Dialectic-of-Enlightenment, Medium},
  abstract = {Since its publication, Max Horkheimer and Theodor W. Adorno's Dialectic of Enlightenment has inspired a wide range of interpretations. In particular, its central claim – that active domination over nature ultimately leads to passive domination by nature – continues to provoke debate. Based on a novel ecological explanation of this reversal, the article identifies two contrasting perspectives in the discussions by Jürgen Habermas, Axel Honneth and Amy Allen, among others. Whereas some critical theorists understand the reversal into domination by nature as the result of human history in general, others regard it as a contingent outcome of specifically modern societies. Finally, the articles argues that both readings attempt to sway the Dialectic of Enlightenment toward a single perspective, while its authors leave the tension between the two unresolved.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{verovshek2025rebuilding,
  author = {Verov{\v{s}}ek, Peter J.},
  title = {Rebuilding the {F}rankfurt {S}chool's Bridge From Theory to Practice: {J}{\"u}rgen {H}abermas's Rehabilitation of Critical Theory},
  journal = {Constellations},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {33},
  number = {1},
  pages = {3--13},
  doi = {10.1111/1467-8675.70001},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Verovshek argues that Habermas' contribution to critical
  theory is best understood as a rehabilitation rather than a repudiation: he
  reconnects critical theory's normative aspirations to an empirically grounded
  account of modernity's communicative structures. This is presented as an
  attempt to rebuild the bridge between theory and emancipatory practice that
  the first generation's pessimism had severed. The article situates this
  rehabilitation within the broader tradition and evaluates its success.

  RELEVANCE: This 2025 article in Constellations provides a synthetic recent
  assessment of Habermas' place in the Frankfurt School tradition, directly
  addressing the research project's question about Habermas' relation to his
  predecessors. It serves as a useful framing piece for the domain's key
  debates.

  POSITION: Sympathetic reconstruction of Habermas' self-positioning within
  the Frankfurt School tradition.
  },
  keywords = {Habermas, Frankfurt-School, rehabilitation, communicative-action, Medium},
  abstract = {The relationship between theory and practice is a key philosophical problem. Since Plato reacted to the sentencing of Socrates to death by separating the contemplative work of theory from the active work of politics, most philosophers—even most political philosophers—have argued that practical applications “are not prerequisites of a theory” (Schmidtz 2006, p. 27). Today, these questions have reemerged, as philosophers and political theorists increasingly have to justify the value of theory to both public officials in order to obtain funding and to students worried about their employment prospects. This has led some political philosophers to claim that they should “have socio-political ambitions for their work” (Bright 2021). Going even further, proponents of public philosophy argue that political thinkers “have a responsibility to take matters of public policy seriously” (Wolff 2011, p. 1). The critical theory tradition associated with the Institut für Sozialforschung (Institute for Social Research, hereafter IfS) in Frankfurt is a touchstone for these debates. Established in 1923 following the worker's uprisings that accompanied the end of the Great War in Germany, the Frankfurt Circle sought to understand why the proletarian revolution had failed. Driven by Karl Marx's project of “ruthless criticism of the existing order” (in Tucker 1978, p. 788), the first generation sought to fulfill Marx's Eleventh Thesis on Feuerbach by building a bridge between theory and practice. However, on its hundredth anniversary, there is widespread agreement that “critical theory today is rarely practiced in the sense intended by its founders” (Ibsen 2022, p. 3). This “domestication” (Thompson 2016) is often attributed to the “neo-idealist” linguistic paradigm Jürgen Habermas introduced in the 1970s. These critics allege that Habermas “abandoned” (Thornhill 2000, p. 173) the practical aspirations of the original paradigm of the Frankfurt School by creating a “philosophy which finds peace within itself… [that] has nothing to do with critical theory” (Rockmore 1989, p. 166). This scholarship presents Habermas as an apostate, who, despite learning from Theodor Adorno, “retreated into a kind of transcendentalism” that sealed critical theory off from practice. Raymond Geuss (1981, p. 94) thus concludes that the Frankfurt School is “better off” insofar as it is “closer to Adorno…than to Habermas,” while other prominent commentators see Habermas's approach as “an insidious ruse” (Butler 1995, p. 56). This article pushes back against this tendency to pit “the ‘good’ Adorno against the ‘bad’ Habermas” (Specter 2019, p. 322). In contrast to the dominant interpretation outlined above, I argue that the Frankfurt School's retreat from practice actually dates back to the first generation itself, which lost its faith in the possibility of political change following the rise of National Socialism. I therefore agree with Martin Jay (2023, p. 15) that the Frankfurt School has consistently “struggled to bridge the gap between radical theory and transformative praxis.” Far from severing the critical theory from practice, I show that Habermas engages in theoretically informed praxis as a public intellectual (see also Verovšek 2021b, 2022, Forthcoming). Whereas the first generation struggled to conceptualize the link between theory and practice after realizing that the proletariat could no longer serve as a vehicle for the revolution, in his work Habermas builds a theoretically consistent bridge between these two spheres via his theory of the public sphere as well as his activities to improve the quality of public debate within it. My argument proceeds on two levels. First, I contend that Habermas reinvigorates Horkheimer's (1972, p. 199) commitment to “theory dominated at every turn by a concern for reasonable conditions of life” by shifting its focus away from economic, psychological, and cultural categories in favor of an institutional approach that takes politics seriously. More specifically, I show that Habermas identifies the modern democratic public sphere (Öffentlichkeit) as the “force within [the concrete historical situation] able to stimulate change” that Horkheimer (1972, pp. 188–243) was ultimately unable to locate. Second, I demonstrate that Habermas has sought to shape practice as “an engaged public intellectual in the very same ‘political public sphere’ that he theorized as a philosopher” (Pensky 2013, p. 31). By highlighting Habermas's understudied Kleine politische Schriften (“short political writings”), my basic thesis is that far from having to be drawn out “into a ‘real’ world from which it otherwise remains isolated” (Whyman 2019), Habermas himself has already applied his critical theory to real politics. In my view, Habermas's philosophy not only provides a bridge between theory and practice but also serves as a model for how philosophers as engaged public I by the practical ambitions of the first generation of the Frankfurt School and the of this original which ultimately into I the bridge that Habermas between theory and practice in his of critical theory on the public sphere of his of political on the as a public intellectual I also a of his as a public on his in the of the In the I of Habermas for having on the kind of of the first I also on how Habermas's to the of theory and practice within the (Bright in political theory and on his work as a public intellectual for his in the tradition of the Frankfurt In his of Social and the of an for Social Horkheimer out a for the which its had to the of the of the worker's Horkheimer pp. the he on into the of that a concern for the of the philosophy at the of its this approach the of the a to bridge the gap between theory and practice by the the for a interpretation of a by its for In order to this Horkheimer p. identifies of “the of the of and the in the of this he is to Marx's of the proletariat as the of of the of The informed of he out in wa